course: dictionary
lesson: 14301
title: Short forms and the letters NT
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: NT is not really a word. It is a short piece of a word, or a set of letters that stands for longer words. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning is right. «I don't like cold porridge.» Here n't is a short form of not, and the apostrophe shows that letters are missing.
explain: NT also stands for longer names. «Darwin is the capital of the NT.» Darwin is in Australia, so NT means Northern Territory. «We read from the NT in RE.» In RE, NT means New Testament, the second part of the Christian Bible. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
explain: When you see NT, check the words around it. A capital city, a place or a country points to Northern Territory. A Bible lesson or a Bible book points to New Testament. A garden, an old house or a charity points to National Trust.
fact: n't is a short form of not, used in words like don't, isn't and can't.
fact: Don't means do not, isn't means is not, and can't means cannot.
fact: NT can stand for New Testament, the second part of the Christian Bible.
fact: NT can stand for Northern Territory, a part of Australia whose capital is Darwin.
fact: NT can stand for the National Trust, a UK charity that looks after old houses, gardens and countryside.
exercise: «I don't like cold porridge.» In don't, what does n't stand for? | now | *not | new | none
exercise: «Darwin is the capital of the NT.» What does NT stand for here? | New Testament | *Northern Territory | Night time | National Trust
exercise: Which word is a short form of do not? | door | dolt | done | *don't
test: «We read from the NT in RE today.» What does NT stand for in this sentence? | *New Testament | Northern Territory | Next Tuesday | National Trust
test: «Won't» is short for will not. What does the n't in won't stand for? | now | *not | nut | new
exam: In «Our class visited the NT and saw Uluru and Darwin», what does NT stand for? | National Trust | New Testament | *Northern Territory | Not Today
